        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks achieved extraordinary control of atoms and a major advance in time and frequency measurement.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye are clear leading pioneers of the optical-lattice-clock concept and its realization.",
        "maturity": "Performance, reproducibility, and scientific utility are thoroughly demonstrated in multiple laboratories.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The eventual balance between conceptual discovery and precision-engineering achievement requires careful judgment."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Yakir Aharonov",
        "Michael Berry"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and geometric phase exposed the physical role of phase and gauge structure with consequences across quantum physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov and Berry are unmistakable names for the two central phenomena, although their discoveries are historically distinct.",
        "maturity": "Both effects have extensive experimental confirmation and broad conceptual influence.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Combining two separate phenomena into one prize-worthy discovery may weaken thematic coherence."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B021",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold-atom simulators gave controlled experimental access to Hubbard-model many-body physics and established quantum simulation as a serious scientific method.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner led landmark experimental work, with Zoller a central theoretical architect; attribution extends beyond three individuals.",
        "maturity": "The platforms are robust and have produced reproducible many-body results across several groups.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Whether the simulated Hubbard problems have yielded sufficiently unique fundamental discoveries beyond platform creation remains open."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
